    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In this paper, I exploit organic user buzz to reveal that investors view\r\npositive user data as a proxy for future user acquisition and company growth.\r\nAfter conducting venture capital investment analysis, I show that companies\r\nthat experience higher degrees of market excitement receive higher levels of\r\ninterest from investors. The more user buzz a startup receives, the more\r\nlikely it is that it will, first, participate in an additional funding round, and,\r\nsecond, receive a higher valuation in the next funding cycle. My analysis also\r\nfinds that venture capitalists are not yet adept at predicting market\r\nexcitement for a particular product at earlier funding stages. Instead, they\r\nare more likely to apply a “spray and pray” early-stage investing approach.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Heng, Wei Ling. (2015). BUZZ: The Impact of Positive User Sentiment on Startup Company Valuations in the USA. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: https://purl.stanford.edu/hw378hh2268",
            "type": "preferred citation",
